These are the different types I have listed so far:

-> Write Never   (read only)

-> Write Once    (publication safety)
   [Update: I d like to disagree with my earlier assesment. This is not about
   publication safety where when you get the pointer its assumed its contents
   are initialized.

   In this case its udnerstood the contents are lazily initialized. The check
   for isInitialized involves a lock+bool or fence+bool either ways its bad
   since you pay it for a gazzilion reads. My goal is to take the bool-fence-lock
   out all of it and replace it with plain vanilla bold reads (yeahhhh!!!) :-)

-> Write Rarely  (mostly readonly but can be written)

-> Concurrent Writers  (different threads/cpus write to different copies of 
   	      	        the same memory and eventually these different copies
			are MERGED!! Merge algorithm can be provided by programmer
			or use std built ins like addition (for perf counters))

-> Write Heavy   (lots of writes)
---> Good Thread Locality (locks are rarely contested but get used by same threads)

---> Bad  Thread Locality (locks ping pong a lot between different threads)

------> Loosely Contested (even though they ping pong between threads at a time 
			   there are not a lot of **SIMULTANEOUS** contenders)
			   < good candidates for lockless algorithms since
			     rollback will be rare and PerThreadLocking breaks down here
			     because you keep paying for lock stealing via signals.

			     Q: Can I improve PTL to know there are contenders and	
			        surrender lock back after first use?
			     A: Are you listening... ? Its rate of lock owner shift
			        we re talking of here NOT #simultaneous lock contenders
			     >

------> Hotly   Contested (the worst of the lot)
			   < cant help you .. fix the code/stripe the lock  otherwise use 
			     adaptive lock with good throttling and NUMA  
			     characteristics >
--------------------------------------------------------------
All except Write Heavy - BTL -> Hot can be optimized for.

Loosely Contested I m thinking of using optimistic concurrency (my fault based one)

Good Thread locality can use your store based per cpu page table locks.

